{
  "refId": "units",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "source": "ISO 80000 / NIST SP 811 Guide for the Use of the International System of Units; exact definitions where the definition is exact, full published value otherwise.",
  "note": "Units of measure for engineering quantities, with conversions to a canonical SI unit per quantity. Factors are exact where the definition is exact (ISO 80000 / NIST SP 811); otherwise they are the full published value, not a rounded one.",
  "revision_date": "2026-09-15",
  "notes": [
    "TEMPERATURE IS AFFINE. Celsius and Fahrenheit have both a factor and an offset; a converter that multiplies only is wrong by 32 degrees at every point. Verified: 0 C naively becomes 0 F instead of 32 F.",
    "A temperature DIFFERENCE converts with the factor only, never the offset. A 50 C rise is 90 F of rise, not 122. Any future delta-temperature field must not reuse the point conversion.",
    "Gauge and absolute pressure are recorded as separate units and are NOT interconverted. The atmospheric datum is a site condition, not a constant, so barg to bara is an engineering decision rather than arithmetic.",
    "Conversion is lossy at the stored precision. 285 psi round-trips through kPa as 285.0006 psi at decimal(12,4). This is why the entered value and unit are preserved alongside the canonical value."
  ],
